# deploy/oracle/.env.example — v21.32: DAB's production settings on the
# Oracle VM. Walkthrough: docs/deploy/oracle-always-free.md, step 8.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env && nano .env
#
# Nothing below is a real secret. Generate each one ON THE SERVER with the
# command shown, and keep a copy of .env in your password manager. Without
# STORAGE_ENCRYPTION_KEY, a restored backup's files cannot be read.
#
# Format: one NAME=value per line. Docker Compose reads this file, so put a
# value with spaces in "double quotes", and a value containing $ in 'single
# quotes' (Compose expands $NAME inside unquoted and double-quoted values).
# Settings not listed here keep the defaults described in the repo-root
# .env.example.

# ── Read by compose.yml ───────────────────────────────────
# The image CI publishes to Docker Hub: <your Docker Hub username>/dab, tag
# :latest or a commit SHA (step 13 uses the SHA to roll back). The tag holds
# an amd64 and an arm64 image, and Docker pulls the arm64 one on Ampere A1.
DAB_IMAGE=YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/dab:latest
# The name whose DNS A record points at this VM. Caddy gets its certificate.
# "localhost" makes Caddy use its own test CA (local trials only).
DOMAIN=dab.example.com

# ── Server ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# compose.yml pins both to these values; they are here so this file lists
# every production setting. TRUST_PROXY=1: one proxy (Caddy) in front of
# Node. Any other value records the wrong IP address in the traffic log
# (Computer-Related Crime Act s.26) and lets the login limiter be dodged.
NODE_ENV=production
TRUST_PROXY=1
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ── Secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# console.anthropic.com → API keys. The account should be on the Commercial
# Terms (docs/privacy/vendors.md §1).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Token-signing keys. Production refuses to start without them.
#   openssl rand -hex 32        (run once for each, and use different values)
JWT_SECRET=
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=
# Encrypts every stored upload (AES-256-GCM). Set it BEFORE the first boot.
# If you added it later, or moved data in from Railway, encrypt the older
# files once and restart (guide, step 9):
#   docker compose exec dab node src/scripts/storage-encrypt.mjs --dry-run
#   docker compose exec dab node src/scripts/storage-encrypt.mjs
#   docker compose restart dab
# Changing or losing the key makes every file saved under it unreadable.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
STORAGE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ── Storage and cost ──────────────────────────────────────
# local = files in the data volume, beside the SQLite database. backup.sh
# copies that volume. With s3, the files would live in a bucket that this
# backup does not cover.
STORAGE_DRIVER=local
# Daily cap on Anthropic calls, all users together.
AI_DAILY_BUDGET=300

# ── Operator access (optional) ────────────────────────────
# Only your own username, or nothing (docs/privacy/README.md).
# TELEMETRY_ADMINS=
# For a monitor that reads /api/metrics.   openssl rand -hex 32
# METRICS_TOKEN=

# ── Legal pages: /privacy, /terms, /contact ───────────────
# Production logs a warning at boot while any of the five required values
# (all except the name) is empty, and the pages say "being set up" instead.
LEGAL_OPERATOR_NAME="Phatcharaphong Siriphatcharakul"
# PDPA s.23: a way to reach the controller. Both are required.
LEGAL_CONTACT_EMAIL=
# One line, or "\n" between lines inside double quotes.
LEGAL_POSTAL_ADDRESS=
# Where the data is stored. Check which Oracle company your Oracle account's
# agreement names, and add it in brackets if counsel wants it named.
LEGAL_HOSTING_PROVIDER="Oracle Cloud Infrastructure"
LEGAL_HOSTING_REGION="Singapore (ap-singapore-1)"
# The PDPA s.28 exception or s.29 safeguard for keeping data in Singapore.
# This is a question for counsel, so fill it in only with counsel's wording.
# The research found that Oracle's data processing agreement covers
# processing but its transfer annex has no clause for Thailand, Singapore or
# ASEAN. Until this is set, the pages say "being set up", which is true. A
# guessed answer would not be.
LEGAL_HOSTING_TRANSFER_BASIS=

# ── Retention ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave every *_RETENTION_DAYS unset. The defaults are the periods the
# Privacy Notice states (docs/privacy/retention-schedule.md §6 says how to
# change one).
